{"transformation": "publish_conclusion", "actor": "claude_fable_5", "args_named": {"conclusion": "concl-004-reconstructed", "inquiry": "inq-004", "conjecture": "conj-004-half-hour-reconstructable", "conclusion_class": "partly_explained", "statement": "PARTLY EXPLAINED, with the observability boundary where the declaration predicted it. The frozen rule selected 2026-06-24 p42 (Constraints GBP1,176,150), which falls inside the 19:00-22:00 window NESO had warned about the previous night and CANCELLED at 13:20; all four of June's dearest half-hours fall inside it. NESO's own notice supplies the bridge: 1,250-1,300 MW of generation was excluded from available system margin BY SYSTEM CONSTRAINTS, against a 1,450-1,900 MW shortfall. At the window's open the dispatchable fleet was at published limits (CCGT headroom 88 MW; pumped storage, nuclear and non-pumped hydro zero). Through the peak the record shows 1,085 MWh of accepted bids across 113 units with ZERO accepted offers, pumped storage held at 0 against a 150 MW schedule, and the large positive money OUTSIDE the BM (GBP1,277,241 over 1,173 MWh, all classified Energy) while BM cashflow is negative. Not determinable from public data: why the Constraints series peaks in p42 rather than p41 where BSAD peaks; the Peterhead gap between ~729 MW metered and ~1,150 MW instructed; and whether the generation NESO excluded from margin is the generation visible being bid down. No transmission boundary is named.", "evidence_digest": "79fd027f771cd2071ab9572fe1ccfc78ffd4be112acf531912fc02197800fc19", "published_at": "2026-08-22T22:31:15Z"}}
